@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — two protocols on the two paths
#
# The first states a condition under which a claim would fail and reports that
# no case answering it has been found. The second states that no condition has
# been devised at all, which is a different finding and must not read like the
# first.
#

pmp:BinaryPredatesItsReportersExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the two-way division is older than the reports of it"@en, "Ob die Zweiteilung älter ist als ihre Berichterstatter"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the naming of two Śākta branches as a right-hand and a left-hand ritual reports a division the Indian material carries, rather than imposing one it does not."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A pre-colonial Indian source that presents dakṣiṇa and vāma as a clean two-way division with a moral polarity, using union with the divine against self-deification as the criterion that separates them. One such passage refutes the claim that the division was imposed rather than found." ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "The claim treats two findings alike that are not alike. In the one, no pre-colonial text divides at all, and the naming invents its object. In the other, a pre-colonial text does announce a two-way division of methods in these very terms, and the naming keeps the pair while attaching a moral charge the text does not carry. The falsification condition below is met by neither, so the protocol returned the same verdict for both — and the second is now the established case (pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods)."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "H. B. Urban, The Power of Tantra: Religion, Sexuality and the Politics of South Asian Studies (I.B. Tauris, London 2010), in the chapter on the sacrifice of desire, at the section on the origins of sexual rites and the right and left-hand paths" ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched for the opposite of the claim: a pre-colonial Indian source carrying the two-way division. Opposition was found and is now in the graph. Urban's account of the Kālikāpurāṇa led to the passages, which were then read in the Shastri translation rather than taken from him; the purāṇa announces the orthodox and heterodox methods as a pair, prescribes the left hand method for particular deities, and closes by saying both have now been stated. Urban also reports that across most Tantric traditions the pair marks a literal against a symbolic reading of the same prohibited substances — a second division, again without moral polarity. What was not found in either is a criterion of union against self-deification, or a division of practitioners into camps."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/saiva-sakta.ttl and from the Kālikāpurāṇa in the Shastri translation" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-09" ;
    skos:note "The state moved from procedure-without-cases past cases-without-deviation and to non-discrimination, and the middle step was deliberately not taken. A case has now been read, so the first state no longer fits; but reporting it as a case without deviation would say the claim survived a test, when what happened is that the test could not tell the two possible worlds apart. The finding is about the wording of the claim and not about the Indian material."@en ;
    skos:note "Three divisions now stand in the graph and no two of them agree: seven ascending ācāras with the left above the right (pmc:AcaraLadder), three dispositions deciding competence (pmc:BhavaClassification), and two methods assigned by the deity worshipped (pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods). That is the shape of the actual answer, and it is more interesting than either yes or no: the material carries divisions in abundance, and none of them is the one the West received."@en ;
    skos:note "What is still not established. The corpus has not been searched exhaustively, the tantras of the Kaula stream have not been opened for this question, and the modern scholarship on the construction of the category has been used only through the one work cited at pm:evidenceFrom. A reformulated claim is owed before this protocol can return anything but non-discrimination. See prima-materia#71."@en .

pmp:SeparativeEndStateExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the separative end state has been examined at all"@en, "Ob der absondernde Endzustand überhaupt geprüft wurde"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment of the path, held here to record that no procedure for failing it has been devised."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration ;
    pm:examinationState pm:noProcedureDevised ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-29" ;
    skos:note "This node exists to prevent a confusion, not to report a result. Nobody has stated a condition under which the claim would count as refuted, and that is a statement about the means of examination and not about the claim. A record that left the protocol out entirely would say the same thing by silence; a record that reported the claim as unsupported would say something else and something false."@en ;
    skos:note "The asymmetry is worth naming because it is easy to mistake for evidence. States of dissolution have measurable correlates and a literature; the end state claimed on the other side has neither instrument nor operationalisation. That is a fact about the instruments. Whoever infers from it that one path is better attested than the other has read the reach of the measuring apparatus as a finding about the paths. See prima-materia#73."@en ;
    skos:note "No pm:falsifiedBy is given, and the omission is the permitted one: the state says outright that no procedure has been devised. Everywhere else its absence would let a claim drift from unexamined to examined-and-unsupported."@en .
